# PROX-04 — RNG on flat internal tori

Date: 2026-09-23

## Theorem-grade positives

For any finite metric sample, RNG contains every MST edge and is connected.
For an epsilon-net in a geodesic space, every RNG edge has length at most
`2 epsilon`.  On a flat `T4`, sufficiently dense RNG stars are therefore local
Euclidean stars.  The RNG condition implies at least 60-degree angular
separation between incident edge directions.  A self-contained spherical-cap
packing argument gives a finite degree bound (`<=34` in the nonsharp audit)
independent of sample size.

So RNG is a genuine parameter-free sparse proximity rule.

## A structured 4D continuum theorem — and a metric obstruction

On the periodic tensor grid `(Z_m)^4`, RNG is exactly the eight axis-neighbor
graph.  The scaled graph operator

\[
h^{-2}\sum_i[f(x+h e_i)+f(x-h e_i)-2f(x)]
\]

converges to the flat 4D Laplacian with `O(h^2)` error.  The deterministic
control obtains an error slope `1.9897`.

But its weighted shortest-path metric does **not** converge to Euclidean
geodesic distance.  For displacement `(h,h,h,h)` the graph distance is `4h`
and Euclidean distance is `2h`, so stretch is exactly `2` at every resolution.

Hence:

> **Dirichlet/Laplacian continuum and shortest-path metric continuum are
> different bridge problems.**

## Random-Haar scout

For iid uniform points on flat `T4`, RNG remains sparse, but the normalized
local direction tensor does not visibly approach `I/4` over `n=80,160,320`;
median Frobenius anisotropy stays around `0.36–0.40`.

This is numerical evidence only.  It blocks a naive claim of pointwise
isotropic Laplacian convergence from bounded-degree RNG.  A future result must
use weighting/averaging, structured populations or a homogenized notion.

## Disposition

`PROX-04 = PARTIAL_PASS_WITH_METRIC_OBSTRUCTION`.

RNG supplies parameter-free sparse connectivity and admits a controlled 4D
operator continuum on structured samples.  It does not yet supply a unique
physical metric or a theorem for iid/Haar populations.
